---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import NuclideRef from "../components/NuclideRef.astro";
import { NUCLIDES, nuclidePage, halfLifeText, fmtTime } from "../lib/nuclides";
import { u } from "../lib/site";

/** ★★ 양전자 방출체와 511 keV — 2026-10-06 소유주 지시 「각 lab 에 전문가 글 3~5편」의 넷째 편.
 *  **숫자를 글자로 적지 않는다** — 양전자 몫(511 keV 세기의 절반)·반가층·감쇠·재생은 전부
 *  자료와 엔진에서 파생시킨다. 이 표에는 β+ 스펙트럼이 없으므로 **양전자 비정은 숫자로 쓰지 않는다.**
 *  ★ 바깥 출처: AAPM Task Group 108 보고서(PET 차폐) — 이름만 든다. 납 앞치마의 「0.5 mm 납 당량」은
 *    진단 X선 쪽의 흔한 등급으로만 언급하고, 투과율은 우리 자료의 반가층으로 계산한다. */
const N = NUCLIDES;
const pct = (x: number, d = 1) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(d)}%`;
const mm = (cm: number, d = 3) => (Number.isFinite(cm) ? (cm * 10).toPrecision(d) : "—");
/* ★ 핵종 이름은 `NuclideRef` 로만 그린다 — 쪽이 있으면 링크, 없으면 이름만(2026-10-06 ④, 지운 77장으로 가는 죽은 링크를 막는다). */
const after = (k: string, s: number) => Math.pow(2, -s / N[k].t_half_s);
const H = 3600, DAY = 86400, YEAR = 31557600;

/** 511 keV 선이 기록된 핵종 — 자료에서 찾는다. 양전자 몫 = 세기/2(소멸 한 번에 광자 둘). */
const ANNIH = Object.keys(N)
  .map((k) => ({ k, l: N[k].lines.find(([e]) => Math.abs(e - 511) < 0.5) }))
  .filter((r) => r.l)
  .map(({ k, l }) => {
    const other = N[k].lines.filter(([e, i]) => Math.abs(e - 511) >= 0.5 && e >= 20 && i > 1)
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])[0];
    return { k, i511: l![1], beta: l![1] / 2, other, g: N[k].gamma_const, t: halfLifeText(N[k]) };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => b.beta - a.beta);
const strong = ANNIH.filter((r) => r.beta > 50);
const weak = ANNIH.filter((r) => r.beta <= 50);
const A = (k: string) => ANNIH.find((r) => r.k === k)!;

/** 차폐 — F-18 은 511 keV 한 줄이라 반가층이 곧 그 에너지의 값이다. */
const sh = (k: string, m: string) => nuclidePage(k).shields.find((s) => s.material === m)!;
const f18Pb = sh("F-18", "lead"), f18W = sh("F-18", "tungsten"), f18C = sh("F-18", "concrete");
const tcPb = sh("Tc-99m", "lead");
const csPb = sh("Cs-137", "lead");
const apronCm = 0.05;
const apronT = Math.pow(2, -apronCm / f18Pb.hvlCm);
const gRatio = N["F-18"].gamma_const / N["Tc-99m"].gamma_const;

/** 시간 — 하루 일과 안의 감쇠와 발생기 재생. */
const f18Day = [1, 2, 4, 8].map((h) => ({ h, f: after("F-18", h * H) }));
const gaRegrow = [1, 2, 4].map((h) => ({ h, f: 1 - after("Ga-68", h * H) }));
const gaTen = fmtTime(10 * N["Ga-68"].t_half_s);
const geYear = after("Ge-68", YEAR);
const na = A("Na-22");
const naOther = na.other ? na.other[0] : NaN;
---
<Base title="Positron emitters and 511 keV shielding"
      description="What the 511 keV line in decay data means and why its intensity can approach 200%, how much more lead annihilation radiation needs than Tc-99m, how fast F-18 and Ga-68 decay through a working day, and why a 511 keV peak alone does not identify a positron emitter. Computed from the nuclide data."
      schema="TechArticle">
  <h1 class="text-[26px] font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-[32px]">
    Positron emitters and 511 keV shielding
  </h1>

  <section class="prose-doc mt-6 max-w-3xl text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <p>
      A positron emitter is, for shielding purposes, a source of 511 keV photons, and it behaves quite
      differently from the gamma emitters that most radiation work is organised around. The photons do
      not come from the nucleus. Their energy is fixed by the electron mass rather than by nuclear
      structure, so every positron emitter shares the same principal line. And the nuclides used in
      medicine have half-lives measured in minutes, so the source changes during the time it takes to
      handle it. This note goes through each of those with figures computed from the nuclide table.
    </p>

    <h2>Annihilation radiation</h2>
    <p>
      In positron decay a proton in the nucleus becomes a neutron and the nucleus emits a positron and
      a neutrino. The positron slows down in the surrounding material, combines with an electron, and
      the pair annihilates. Almost always the result is two photons of 511 keV each — the rest energy of
      the electron — travelling in nearly opposite directions. The photons therefore originate where the
      positron stopped, a short distance from the decaying atom, and the source of the radiation is in
      effect the vial wall, the syringe, or the tissue around the injection site.
    </p>
    <p>
      Positron emission always competes with electron capture, which leads to the same daughter without
      emitting a positron, and positron emission is only possible when the decay energy exceeds twice the
      electron rest energy. The share of decays that go by positron emission is a property of each
      nuclide, and it can be read straight from the decay data.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the 511 keV line in decay data</h2>
    <p>
      Decay data record annihilation radiation as a line at 511 keV with an intensity per hundred
      decays. Because each positron gives two photons, the intensity can approach 200%, and half of it is
      the number of positrons per hundred decays. The nuclides in this table with a recorded 511 keV
      line are below.
    </p>
    <div class="pin-first mt-3" tabindex="0">
    <table class="lg:min-w-[42rem]">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Nuclide</th><th>Half-life</th><th>511 keV photons per 100 decays</th>
            <th>Positrons per 100 decays</th>
            <th class="hidden lg:table-cell">Other main line (keV)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ANNIH.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td><NuclideRef k={r.k} /></td>
            <td>{r.t}</td>
            <td>{r.i511.toPrecision(3)}</td>
            <td>{r.beta.toPrecision(3)}</td>
            <td class="hidden lg:table-cell">{r.other ? r.other[0].toFixed(0) : "—"}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px]">
      Positrons per hundred decays are half the recorded 511 keV intensity. The last column is the most
      intense other photon line above 20 keV, where there is one.
    </p>
    <p>
      {strong.map((r) => r.k).join(", ")} are positron emitters in the practical sense: most of their
      decays produce a positron. F-18 emits nothing else above 20 keV, so its photon field is
      annihilation radiation and nothing more. Ga-68 adds a weak line at
      {" "}{A("Ga-68").other ? A("Ga-68").other![0].toFixed(0) : "—"} keV. Na-22 adds a
      {" "}{naOther.toFixed(0)} keV photon in almost every decay, which is why Na-22 is a long-lived
      reference for 511 keV and also a source of true-coincidence summing when the two photons arrive
      together in a detector close to the source. For {weak.map((r) => r.k).join(", ")} the positron branch is
      minor, and their 511 keV line is a small feature beside their other emissions.
    </p>

    <h2>Lead, tungsten and concrete at 511 keV</h2>
    <p>
      F-18 has a single line, so the half-value layers computed for its spectrum are the half-value
      layers of 511 keV photons themselves: {mm(f18Pb.hvlCm)} mm of lead, {mm(f18W.hvlCm)} mm of tungsten
      and {mm(f18C.hvlCm)} mm of concrete, with a tenth-value layer in lead of
      {" "}{mm(f18Pb.tvlCm)} mm. Compared with the sources nuclear medicine was built around, that is a
      different order of shielding. The lead half-value layer for Tc-99m is {mm(tcPb.hvlCm, 2)} mm, so
      each halving costs {(f18Pb.hvlCm / tcPb.hvlCm).toFixed(0)} times as much lead at 511 keV, and F-18
      has an air kerma rate constant {gRatio.toFixed(1)} times that of Tc-99m per becquerel. Even
      Cs-137, at {mm(csPb.hvlCm)} mm, is not far above it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two practical consequences follow. Syringe and vial shields for positron emitters are made of
      tungsten and are much thicker than their Tc-99m counterparts, because tungsten's higher density
      achieves the same attenuation in a size that can still be handled. And protective aprons designed
      for diagnostic X-rays do little at this energy: a layer of {apronCm * 10} mm of lead, a common
      lead-equivalence rating for such aprons, transmits {pct(apronT, 0)} of a narrow 511 keV beam on
      the half-value layer above. Lead-equivalence is in any case specified at X-ray tube energies, and
      aprons made from lead-free composites can do worse at 511 keV than their rating suggests.
    </p>
    <p>
      The figures here are narrow-beam values. For room shielding, where scattered photons reach the
      point of interest around and through the barrier, broad-beam transmission data apply; the AAPM
      Task Group 108 report on PET and PET/CT shielding gives them for lead, concrete and iron, and also
      takes account of the attenuation of the photons in a patient's own body. The
      <a href={u("/gamma-shielding/")} class="underline"> gamma shielding calculator</a> here includes a
      buildup factor for a point source and is a first estimate, not a room design.
    </p>

    <h2>Decay through a working day</h2>
    <p>
      With half-lives of {halfLifeText(N["F-18"])} for F-18 and {halfLifeText(N["Ga-68"])} for Ga-68, the
      activity in hand changes noticeably between dispensing and use. For F-18:
    </p>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      {f18Day.map((d, i) => (
        <li>after {d.h} hour{d.h === 1 ? "" : "s"}, {pct(d.f)} of the activity remains{i === f18Day.length - 1 ? "." : ";"}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      This works in both directions. A dose ordered for a time of injection has to be dispensed with
      more activity than will be given, by a factor that grows exponentially with the delay, and a
      delay in the schedule raises the activity needed from the dispensing laboratory accordingly. On the other side, F-18 waste only
      needs to be held: after 24 hours {pct(after("F-18", DAY), 4)} of the activity remains, so storage
      for decay disposes of it within days. The
      <a href={u("/decay/")} class="underline"> decay calculator</a> gives the exact factor for any
      interval.
    </p>

    <h3>The Ge-68/Ga-68 generator</h3>
    <p>
      Ga-68 is usually obtained from a generator in which its parent, Ge-68
      ({halfLifeText(N["Ge-68"])}), is held on a column. Ge-68 has no photon line in this table; the
      radiation from a generator is the annihilation radiation of the Ga-68 growing on it. After an
      elution removes the Ga-68, it grows back to {pct(gaRegrow[0].f, 0)} of its equilibrium activity in
      one hour, {pct(gaRegrow[1].f, 0)} in two and {pct(gaRegrow[2].f, 0)} in four, and is fully back
      within {gaTen}. The parent sets the generator's working life: after one year a generator retains
      {" "}{pct(geYear, 0)} of its Ge-68, and the Ga-68 it can deliver falls in proportion. The same
      parent–daughter pair is used in sealed form, as Ge-68 phantoms for PET calibration, where the
      daughter is permanently in equilibrium.
    </p>

    <h2>A 511 keV peak is not an identification</h2>
    <p>
      In gamma spectrometry a peak at 511 keV does not by itself show that a positron emitter is
      present. Any photon above twice the electron rest energy can create an electron–positron pair in
      the detector or in the material around it, and the positron's annihilation then produces 511 keV
      photons. High-energy gamma emitters, cosmic radiation and the shielding of the spectrometer all
      contribute a 511 keV peak in this way, and it is present in most background spectra. The
      annihilation peak is also visibly wider than neighbouring gamma peaks, because the motion of the
      electrons involved spreads the photon energies slightly. A positron emitter is identified by its
      other lines where it has them, by the decay of the 511 keV peak with the right half-life, or by
      coincidence between the two back-to-back photons — the principle on which positron emission
      tomography is built.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits of these figures</h2>
    <p>
      The positron itself is not modelled here. This table carries no positron spectrum for these
      nuclides, so nothing on this site gives positron ranges, and the point-source figures assume all
      annihilation happens at the source. Bremsstrahlung from positrons, three-photon annihilation and
      the attenuation of the photons in the source container are also outside these figures.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <ul class="my-3 space-y-1">
      <li><a href={u("/gamma-shielding/")} class="underline">Gamma shielding</a> — dose rate through a
        chosen thickness, with buildup.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/alara/")} class="underline">Time, distance, shielding</a> — stay time and the
        inverse square law for a handling task.</li>
      <li><a href={u("/check-sources/")} class="underline">Nuclides for check and calibration
        sources</a> — Na-22 and Ge-68 among the others.</li>
      <li><NuclideRef k="F-18" />, <NuclideRef k="Ga-68" />
        {" "}and <NuclideRef k="Na-22" /> — the full data pages.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
